## Why This Is Asked
This question tests cross-platform native module design, real-time constraints, memory management, and safe bridging with JavaScript.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time audio pipeline, native threads, DSP paths, and cross-platform bridging
- Lock-free data structures, sample-rate negotiation, backpressure handling, and deterministic scheduling

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal API usage sketch
import { startEngine, feedInput, onProcessed, stopEngine } from 'NativeAudio';

startEngine({ sampleRate: 48000, channels: 1 });
feedInput(inputBuffer, length);
onProcessed((processedBuffer) => { /* handle processed audio */ });
stopEngine();
```

## Architecture Considerations
- **Threading**: Run audio processing on a dedicated high-priority thread (real-time priority on Android, a dedicated dispatch queue on iOS) separate from the JS thread to avoid blocking.
- **Memory**: Use lock-free ring buffers and pre-allocated buffer pools to ensure zero allocations on the hot path.
- **Backpressure**: Implement a bounded queue with clear semantics for what happens when the input buffer fills—either drop frames, signal overflow, or block gracefully based on the use case.
- **Sample-rate changes**: Negotiate sample rate at engine start; if the device doesn't support the requested rate, fall back to the nearest supported rate and notify JS.
- **Fallback**: Detect DSP capability at runtime; if unavailable, either gracefully degrade or expose a clear error indicating the device lacks required hardware support.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical offline-first data handling in React Native. It probes AsyncStorage usage, hook design, and clear cache invalidation logic that affects UX.

## Key Concepts
- AsyncStorage caching with TTL
- Custom React hook design: useCachedQuery
- Offline-first UX and error handling
- FlatList integration for data rendering

## Code Example
```javascript
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import AsyncStorage from '@react-native-async-storage/async-storage';

export function useCachedQuery(key, fetchFn, ttl) {
  const [data, setData] = useState(null);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);

  const load = async () => {
    setLoading(true);
    try {
      const cached = await AsyncStorage.getItem(key);
      if (cached) {
        const { value, ts } = JSON.parse(cached);
        if (Date.now() - ts < ttl) {
          setData(value);
          setLoading(false);
          return;
        }
      }
      const fresh = await fetchFn();
      await AsyncStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify({ value: fresh, ts: Date.now() }));
      setData(fresh);
    } catch (e) {
      setError(e);
    } finally {
      setLoading(false);
    }
  };

  useEffect(() => { load(); }, [key]);
  return { data, loading, error, refetch: load };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adjust TTL to balance freshness and offline availability?
- How would you test offline-first behavior in a RN app?

## Why This Is Asked
Gauges candidate ability to architect a small, realistic feature with performance thoughts and React hooks.

## Key Concepts
- In-memory indexing for fast, offline search
- Debounce input and memoize results
- Component memoization to avoid re-renders

## Code Example
```javascript
const EMOJIS = [
  {char:'😀', name:'grinning'},
  {char:'🎉', name:'party'},
  {char:'🚀', name:'rocket'}
];

function useEmojiSearch(query){
  const [q, setQ] = React.useState(query);
  const [debounced, setDebounced] = React.useState(query);
  React.useEffect(() => {
    const t = setTimeout(() => setDebounced(q), 150);
    return () => clearTimeout(t);
  }, [q]);
  const results = React.useMemo(
    () => EMOJIS.filter(e => e.name.toLowerCase().includes(debounced.toLowerCase())),
    [debounced]
  );
  return { query: q, setQuery: setQ, results };
}
```

```javascript
function EmojiPicker(){
  const { query, setQuery, results } = useEmojiSearch('');
  return (
    <>
      <TextInput value={query} onChangeText={setQuery} placeholder="Search emoji" />
      <FlatList
        data={results}
        horizontal
        keyExtractor={e => e.name}
        renderItem={({ item }) => (
          <Text accessibilityLabel={item.name}>{item.char}</Text>
        )}
      />
    </>
  );
}
```

